In 1965, incensed by the auto industry's lack of concern,

231
00:16:48,440 --> 00:16:51,880
consumer advocate Ralph Nader published Unsafe at Any Speed,

232
00:16:52,260 --> 00:16:53,960
which chronicled the state of car safety.

233
00:16:56,200 --> 00:17:01,120
Pressed into action, the government created the Federal Motor Vehicle and Highway Safety Agency

234
00:17:01,121 --> 00:17:04,480
and passed both the National Traffic and Motor Vehicle Safety Act

235
00:17:04,481 --> 00:17:07,080
and the Highway Safety Act of 1966,

236
00:17:07,580 --> 00:17:10,100
which established standards for all vehicles.

237
00:17:11,100 --> 00:17:15,880
In 1967, the first federal motor vehicle safety standard was established.

238
00:17:16,040 --> 00:17:17,280
That was for seatbelt anchorages.

239
00:17:17,840 --> 00:17:21,280
But within 10 months, it was followed by 17 more standards.

240
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:25,540
Everything from the sequence in which you went from park to reverse

241
00:17:25,541 --> 00:17:28,980
to neutral to drive to windshield washing systems

242
00:17:28,981 --> 00:17:35,840
to defrosting systems to requirements for hydraulic brakes, tire requirements, padding in the vehicle.

243
00:17:36,420 --> 00:17:41,580
Boom, just in a period of 10 months, from no federal safety standards to 18 new standards.

244
00:17:42,380 --> 00:17:49,680
With new government supervision and funding, scientists around the country began work on test programs to improve car safety.

245
00:17:50,020 --> 00:17:56,460
Some of the most spectacular were the newly devised crash tests, where new cars were wrecked to see how well they held up.

246
00:17:56,461 --> 00:17:59,960
Crash testing has been around for quite some time.

247
00:17:59,961 --> 00:18:06,160
There's some footage that actually shows a crash test where the person is actually driving the vehicle.

248
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:07,800
So it's not a very fast one.

249
00:18:08,380 --> 00:18:09,720
It runs into the barrier.

250
00:18:12,200 --> 00:18:16,300
But the crash tests of the late 1960s were considerably more complex.

251
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:21,360
Engineers designed a strange array of catapults, sleds, and solid barriers

252
00:18:21,361 --> 00:18:26,720
to test everything from door strength to the effectiveness of seatbelts.

253
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:32,040
We want to design features in vehicles that can protect an occupant.

254
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:35,900
We need to be able to test them in a controlled environment

255
00:18:35,901 --> 00:18:39,120
and ensure that they're doing what we want them to do.

256
00:18:39,121 --> 00:18:43,140
And that's really where the concept of a crash test comes into play.

257
00:18:44,460 --> 00:18:49,200
Automotive scientists borrowed an idea first used by the Air Force to test ejection seats.

258
00:18:49,201 --> 00:18:51,840
and the crash test dummy was born.

259
00:18:53,840 --> 00:18:58,680
By the 1960s, these sophisticated lifelike mannequins had embedded sensors

260
00:18:58,681 --> 00:19:02,080
and were able to measure the forces experienced during collisions.

261
00:19:03,020 --> 00:19:07,080
In the 1960s and going into the 1970s,

262
00:19:08,100 --> 00:19:11,440
General Motors, working through Wayne State University,

263
00:19:11,920 --> 00:19:15,420
began to develop more human-like crash test dummies.

264
00:19:15,421 --> 00:19:22,600
In the mid-1970s, General Motors came out with the Hybrid 3 dummy.

265
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:31,160
The Hybrid 3 dummy is generally considered the most human-like or the most biofidelic crash test dummy that we have.

266
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:37,880
What we're looking at here is a cutaway display version of a Hybrid 3 dummy.

267
00:19:38,280 --> 00:19:43,180
This particular Hybrid 3 dummy is shaped in size to represent an average-sized male.

268
00:19:43,181 --> 00:19:48,180
He's about 5 foot 9 inches tall if he could stand up and weighs about 170 pounds.

269
00:19:48,181 --> 00:19:57,180
The hybrid 3 family includes smaller dummies to represent the population spectrum as well as larger versions to represent bigger people than this dummy here.

270
00:19:57,181 --> 00:20:02,180
The dummy is made of different kinds of plastic and metal and rubber.

271
00:20:02,181 --> 00:20:08,180
Starting with the head, we've got three little sensors here, these little red things that measure motion of the head.

272
00:20:08,181 --> 00:20:12,180
We call them accelerometers because what they really measure is acceleration.

273
00:20:12,181 --> 00:20:15,100
Moving down into the chest, you can see that the dummy has ribs.

274
00:20:15,900 --> 00:20:19,180
These ribs are made of metal, and they've got a plastic material on the back

275
00:20:19,181 --> 00:20:24,280
to make the movement of the ribs more like movement of ribs in a human being.

276
00:20:24,720 --> 00:20:29,880
And we have sensors attached to the back of the dummy's spine that also measure acceleration.

277
00:20:30,660 --> 00:20:34,320
We generally collect measurements from about 30 different sensors,

278
00:20:34,640 --> 00:20:38,780
and all of those measurements are recorded by the data recorder,

279
00:20:39,060 --> 00:20:41,940
which is sitting inside the dummy's chest here.

280
00:20:42,180 --> 00:20:48,080
With these new tools, engineers performed thousands of crash tests.

281
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:51,400
Almost all were filmed using special high-speed cameras

282
00:20:51,401 --> 00:20:55,300
that allowed researchers to scientifically study every nuance of a car crash.

283
00:20:55,680 --> 00:20:59,560
The forces that they witnessed ranged from dangerous to deadly.

284
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:06,440
I've heard people make statements that they're able to brace themselves in a collision.

285
00:21:06,700 --> 00:21:10,420
They understand the collision forces are so high that you cannot bench press

286
00:21:10,421 --> 00:21:12,780
five times your body weight.

287
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:14,280
It could be ten times your body weight,

288
00:21:14,380 --> 00:21:15,440
twenty times your body weight.

289
00:21:15,820 --> 00:21:17,960
So these collision forces are that high.

290
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:21,160
By the early 1970s,

291
00:21:21,440 --> 00:21:23,240
the best protection against these forces

292
00:21:23,241 --> 00:21:24,540
was still the seatbelt.

293
00:21:25,960 --> 00:21:29,480
Unfortunately, less than 25% of the population wore them,

294
00:21:29,820 --> 00:21:32,160
a major factor as the nation's annual death toll

295
00:21:32,161 --> 00:21:35,140
hit an all-time high, over 55,000.

296
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:40,060
Scientists in the late 1960s

297
00:21:40,061 --> 00:21:44,840
realizing that drivers and passengers weren't buckling up, began work on a restraining system

298
00:21:44,841 --> 00:21:46,627
that worked automatically.

299
00:21:46,628 --> 00:21:51,300
In the early 1970s, General Motors began testing the new technology,

300
00:21:51,920 --> 00:21:54,148
airbags that deployed upon impact.

301
00:21:54,149 --> 00:21:57,620
Powered by small explosive charges, the bags inflated

302
00:21:57,621 --> 00:22:01,800
quickly enough to provide a soft barrier between the occupant and the car's interior.

303
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:09,860
Tragically, the bags inflated so quickly, they occasionally killed small women and children.

304
00:22:10,060 --> 00:22:17,060
Over the next 30 years, automakers refined the airbag deployment system.

305
00:22:17,061 --> 00:22:20,060
Yet some of the newest technology was there from the very beginning.

306
00:22:20,061 --> 00:22:25,060
The very first generation of airbags were actually dual-speed airbags.

307
00:22:25,061 --> 00:22:30,060
The ones that General Motors put out in its test fleet way back in 1973 had dual inflation speeds.

308
00:22:30,061 --> 00:22:34,060
They went off at a lower force at a crash speed of 12 miles an hour

309
00:22:34,061 --> 00:22:37,060
and a much bigger force at a crash speed of 18 miles an hour.

310
00:22:37,061 --> 00:22:43,460
Now, it's ironic that 30 years later, we're finally going back to do exactly the same thing again.

311
00:22:44,540 --> 00:22:50,040
In the mid-1980s, anti-lock brake systems were created to give the driver more control.

312
00:22:50,940 --> 00:22:56,900
Just before a crash, drivers typically stop on the brakes, sending the car into an uncontrollable skid.

313
00:22:57,780 --> 00:23:01,060
The anti-lock system increases and decreases the brake pressure,

314
00:23:01,740 --> 00:23:04,940
allowing for the maximum amount of braking without locking the wheels

315
00:23:04,941 --> 00:23:07,200
and sending the car careening off the road.

316
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:12,800
But despite tremendous leaps forward in making cars safer,

317
00:23:13,220 --> 00:23:17,100
both government and industry realized better cars were only half the battle.

318
00:23:17,101 --> 00:23:20,500
The roads themselves would undergo a long and winding evolution

319
00:23:20,501 --> 00:23:23,780
to make them safer and ultimately save lives.

320
00:23:23,781 --> 00:23:36,840
Between 1900 and 1953, over 1 million Americans had died in car crashes, nearly double the combined American deaths in both world wars.

321
00:23:38,920 --> 00:23:42,020
Car crashes will return on Modern Marvels.

322
00:23:44,340 --> 00:23:46,523
Roughly 30% of all fatalities on U.S.

323
00:23:46,524 --> 00:23:51,360
roads happen not when two vehicles collide, but when a single car leaves the road.

324
00:23:51,361 --> 00:23:57,140
engineers at the Texas Transportation Institute are working to change that

325
00:23:57,141 --> 00:24:01,060
today they are testing a new bridge railing

326
00:24:01,061 --> 00:24:05,320
meant to keep out of control drivers from leaving the road and ending up another statistic

327
00:24:05,321 --> 00:24:08,400
the test will involve a speeding pickup truck

328
00:24:08,401 --> 00:24:13,160
1200 feet behind me today is the vehicle that will be impacting this rail

329
00:24:13,161 --> 00:24:17,840
what will be guiding this vehicle into the rail will be a cable guidance system

330
00:24:17,841 --> 00:24:21,300
and pulling the vehicle will be the tow truck you see behind me

331
00:24:21,301 --> 00:24:24,540
The pickup will be traveling 60 miles an hour into the rail.

332
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:31,740
The test crew makes last-minute preparations and checks the high-speed cameras, which will capture all the action.

333
00:24:33,540 --> 00:24:36,600
Finally, the tow truck driver is signaled to begin the test.

334
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:59,920
At first glance, the test seems to be a resounding success.

335
00:25:00,600 --> 00:25:03,440
Upon impact, the truck's path is redirected onto the track.

336
00:25:04,920 --> 00:25:07,400
It did contain and redirect this vehicle.

337
00:25:07,960 --> 00:25:14,040
If this had been on a bridge or an overpass, it kept it out of the traffic that would have been below the bridge

338
00:25:14,041 --> 00:25:15,900
or had it been over a body of water.

339
00:25:16,020 --> 00:25:18,320
It kept that vehicle on the roadway and redirected it.

340
00:25:18,321 --> 00:25:21,300
So in terms of performance, this rail was a success.

341
00:25:22,580 --> 00:25:25,040
But the test vehicle tells a different story.

342
00:25:25,800 --> 00:25:29,740
We got some damage on the pickup truck that we certainly didn't anticipate today.

343
00:25:30,040 --> 00:25:32,260
And this damage is caused by the rail.

344
00:25:33,480 --> 00:25:37,760
And the contact surface of the railing with the vehicle was probably contributory

345
00:25:37,761 --> 00:25:41,560
to making the wheel go into the compartment

346
00:25:41,561 --> 00:25:45,400
and create less living room, we call it, inside the vehicle.

347
00:25:45,401 --> 00:25:49,800
And this does this by the floorboards going in towards the occupant compartment,

348
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:56,160
and we're fearful that this would cause lower limb injury and more serious injury in a driver or a passenger.

349
00:25:58,160 --> 00:26:02,040
The test crew reviews the high-speed video and finds the problem.

350
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:06,720
The joints where the railing is held together snag the vehicle,

351
00:26:07,160 --> 00:26:10,180
causing the crash deceleration to be much more violent than expected.

352
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:12,840
The rail will need to be redesigned.

353
00:26:13,800 --> 00:26:17,540
There's nothing like running a test. We can do a lot of analysis.

354
00:26:18,180 --> 00:26:23,820
We even do a lot of computer simulation now these days, but the proof is always in the pudding.

355
00:26:25,100 --> 00:26:29,520
Today, government agencies and research institutes take road safety very seriously.

356
00:26:30,720 --> 00:26:32,740
But throughout the first half of the 20th century,

357
00:26:33,820 --> 00:26:37,820
lawmakers adhered to a philosophy that was both pragmatic and dangerous.

358
00:26:37,821 --> 00:26:45,020
one of my colleagues referred to it as the nut behind the wheel philosophy really up until the

359
00:26:45,021 --> 00:26:52,140
1960s there was not a lot of attention given to vehicles that ran off the road there was kind of a

360
00:26:52,141 --> 00:26:58,160
widely held philosophy that if someone ran off the road it was their fault they did something wrong

361
00:26:58,161 --> 00:27:05,300
and so they could face the consequences drivers at the turn of the 20th century never knew what

362
00:27:05,301 --> 00:27:06,940
to expect when they took to the road.

363
00:27:06,941 --> 00:27:10,420
In some areas, asphalt and concrete was the norm.

364
00:27:10,421 --> 00:27:14,340
But most of the nation was a loose collection of dirt roads.

365
00:27:14,341 --> 00:27:18,040
Roads both help the accident situation

366
00:27:18,041 --> 00:27:22,500
in the early days and contributed to it. The extent to which they held down speeds,

367
00:27:22,501 --> 00:27:26,420
obviously people weren't going fast enough to get into real serious crashes.

368
00:27:26,421 --> 00:27:30,100
On the other hand, the quality of the roads,

369
00:27:30,101 --> 00:27:34,260
the geometric design of the roads, the shoulders, the curvatures,

370
00:27:34,261 --> 00:27:37,840
very primitive and so when you did get into trouble and ran off the road you

371
00:27:37,841 --> 00:27:39,600
were very likely to run into a tree

372
00:27:39,601 --> 00:27:43,760
or into a culvert that hadn't been properly engineered for automobiles

373
00:27:43,761 --> 00:27:49,020
deadly hairpin turns at the end of long straightaways

374
00:27:49,021 --> 00:27:52,460
or drop-offs on the side of the highway were common killers

375
00:27:52,461 --> 00:27:57,260
in the early 1950s the spread of the interstate highway system under

376
00:27:57,261 --> 00:27:58,360
President Eisenhower

377
00:27:58,361 --> 00:28:01,420
led to an outpouring of funds for research and testing

378
00:28:01,421 --> 00:28:08,740
To make the roads less dangerous, the Federal Highway Administration was created to regulate their construction and safety.

379
00:28:10,020 --> 00:28:13,520
One of the first and simplest road improvements was the clear zone.

380
00:28:14,340 --> 00:28:20,220
We realized that people that were leaving the road, it was not always their fault.

381
00:28:20,221 --> 00:28:23,260
We needed to try and keep them safe.

382
00:28:23,780 --> 00:28:31,160
And one of the most basic things that we did was begin providing what became known as a clear zone or a clear recovery area.

383
00:28:31,420 --> 00:28:35,920
on the side of the road.

384
00:28:35,921 --> 00:28:40,920
When space wasn't available, road builders struggled to shield natural hazards too large

385
00:28:40,921 --> 00:28:42,180
or costly to remove.

386
00:28:42,181 --> 00:28:48,060
From the Clear Zone concept came a wide range of physical restraining and impact devices

387
00:28:48,061 --> 00:28:51,560
meant to keep the cars on the road.

388
00:28:51,561 --> 00:28:54,720
But natural hazards weren't the only problem.

389
00:28:54,721 --> 00:29:00,040
Any object on the side of the road can become a potential killer.

390
00:29:00,041 --> 00:29:04,860
During high-speed impacts, signposts rip through cars and bring them to a lethal stop,

391
00:29:05,320 --> 00:29:11,240
while a roadside mailbox can decapitate a driver as efficiently as a guillotine.

392
00:29:13,520 --> 00:29:18,300
In the 1960s, we began to develop breakaway sign supports,

393
00:29:18,860 --> 00:29:24,460
and one of the most successful devices that is still in use today has become known as the slip base.

394
00:29:24,461 --> 00:29:27,020
It was developed here at Texas Transportation Institute.

395
00:29:27,021 --> 00:29:37,560
It essentially provides a slip plane that allows the pole or sign support to release from its foundation and rotate up and over an impacting vehicle.

396
00:29:38,500 --> 00:29:42,100
Other safety devices, such as guardrails, developed over time.

397
00:29:43,280 --> 00:29:46,960
Some of the earliest were simple poles connected by steel rope.

398
00:29:47,620 --> 00:29:49,300
Often they did as much damage as good.

399
00:29:49,301 --> 00:29:57,020
Throughout the 50s and 60s, the designs were improved to keep pace with the changes in vehicle's speed and design.

400
00:29:58,540 --> 00:30:04,740
Once the actual guardrail design was brought to a point where it was functioning well in the field,

401
00:30:05,160 --> 00:30:08,380
there was now this new problem that surfaced with the guardrail ends.

402
00:30:08,720 --> 00:30:14,340
And we found that these stand-up, or blunt ends, as they became known, could spear a vehicle.

403
00:30:14,341 --> 00:30:21,893
And we actually have some instances where the guardrail beam element actually went through the engine compartment and through the passenger compartment and out the

404
00:30:21,894 --> 00:30:22,820
rear of the vehicle.

405
00:30:23,340 --> 00:30:25,340
So obviously this was a very nasty problem.

406
00:30:25,840 --> 00:30:29,760
And there was a lot of research devoted to this in the 1970s.

407
00:30:30,580 --> 00:30:33,800
One early remedy involved bending the rail ends down.

408
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:36,820
But that caused vaulting or launch problems.

409
00:30:36,821 --> 00:30:43,440
Another solution implemented by many states in the early 1980s used guardrail ends called

410
00:30:43,441 --> 00:30:45,520
breakaway cable terminals.

411
00:30:45,521 --> 00:30:50,620
Though better in some instances, when struck, the railing twisted erratically, occasionally

412
00:30:50,621 --> 00:30:53,220
entering the car, killing the occupants.

413
00:30:53,221 --> 00:30:58,300
The Texas Transportation Institute developed a better solution in the late 1980s, when

414
00:30:58,301 --> 00:31:05,360
after exhaustive research and testing, engineers designed the ET-2000, an energy absorbent

415
00:31:05,361 --> 00:31:06,061
end cap.

416
00:31:06,062 --> 00:31:12,720
It was the first energy absorbing guardrail end treatment and essentially it looks

417
00:31:12,721 --> 00:31:18,660
like a box that's placed on the end of the guardrail and what that box does is it dissipates

418
00:31:18,661 --> 00:31:20,492
the energy of the vehicle.

419
00:31:20,493 --> 00:31:25,000
The guardrail beam element is first flattened and then deflected

420
00:31:25,001 --> 00:31:30,380
out of the way and this flattening and deflecting process absorbs the energy of the vehicle and

421
00:31:30,381 --> 00:31:32,208
brings it to a safe stop.

422
00:31:32,209 --> 00:31:36,740
Today there are railings, decelerators and barriers for nearly

423
00:31:36,741 --> 00:31:38,363
every roadside obstacle.

424
00:31:38,364 --> 00:31:42,960
From simple sand and water filled containers to break away railings.

425
00:31:44,200 --> 00:31:49,000
Still, engineers continue to test and improve the technology in the name of road safety.

426
00:31:49,860 --> 00:31:54,720
But even the best built railings can't prevent crashes. And when they do happen,

427
00:31:54,721 --> 00:31:59,520
it's the men and women of the nation's fire departments that are there to pick up the pieces.

428
00:32:00,380 --> 00:32:07,300
A study by Harvard University estimates that drivers using cell phones account for about

429
00:32:07,301 --> 00:32:13,100
6% of highway collisions, killing 2,600 Americans every year.

430
00:32:13,101 --> 00:32:18,020
Car crashes will return on Modern Marvels.

431
00:32:18,021 --> 00:32:23,360
In spite of the great strides forward in both car and road safety, over 3.4 million Americans

432
00:32:23,361 --> 00:32:27,780
are injured in motor vehicle crashes every year.

433
00:32:27,781 --> 00:32:31,300
immediate help to victims is literally a matter of life and death.

434
00:32:32,660 --> 00:32:37,520
The combination of modern rescue and extrication equipment, the tools and the training and the

435
00:32:37,521 --> 00:32:42,420
professional people that use them, and the modern medical procedures that are now permitted in the

436
00:32:42,421 --> 00:32:46,720
field by paramedics, there's no question in my mind but what they're saving a lot of lives.

437
00:32:47,220 --> 00:32:51,140
And I say that with the experience of having lived through it before they came along.

438
00:32:52,360 --> 00:32:56,420
Today, fire crews arrive on the scene with a full arsenal of extrication gear.

439
00:32:57,780 --> 00:33:00,940
Right in front of me is some of the equipment that we have on this paramedic unit.

440
00:33:01,940 --> 00:33:04,720
This is known to a lot of people as the jaws of life.

441
00:33:05,200 --> 00:33:08,880
We have the spreaders, which are used to get into a door.

442
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:11,460
These are the cutters.

443
00:33:11,600 --> 00:33:14,220
We usually use the cutters for cutting uprights.

444
00:33:14,560 --> 00:33:16,800
Also, we use this to cut the hinges off the doors.

445
00:33:17,500 --> 00:33:20,320
Basically, this has about 60,000 pounds of cutting force.

446
00:33:21,580 --> 00:33:25,280
The ram unit is actually used if we have a person entrapped in a vehicle.

447
00:33:25,281 --> 00:33:28,220
In most cases, it's usually the dash that's entrapped on the patient.

448
00:33:28,500 --> 00:33:32,940
We can actually use the ram unit to push the dash away from the victim.

449
00:33:36,200 --> 00:33:41,580
Since the very first car wreck, accident victims have been at the mercy of emergency medical crews.

450
00:33:42,780 --> 00:33:51,140
Well into the 1930s, firemen with no medical training arrived at accident scenes with only pry bars to dig the victim out of the twisted metal.

451
00:33:51,141 --> 00:33:56,140
In the early years of my career, when we were equipped with fairly crude and rudimentary equipment,

452
00:33:56,280 --> 00:34:00,240
we'd roll up on the scene of an auto crash with victims that were pinned in the vehicle,

453
00:34:00,500 --> 00:34:03,760
and we knew that we'd be able to save their lives if we could just get them out.

454
00:34:04,100 --> 00:34:09,580
But sometimes it would take so long to basically remove the vehicle from those people that they died,

455
00:34:09,780 --> 00:34:11,460
and it was very difficult for us to take.

456
00:34:15,160 --> 00:34:19,400
Some of the greatest advances in emergency medical treatment would happen not on the road,

